// @proven: 2
// @failed: 0
// @unknown: 0
// `x < y` implies `x + 1 <= y` over the INTEGERS, but not over the rationals (y = x + 0.5).
//
// This fixture used to lock the opposite verdict. Fourier-Motzkin decides feasibility over
// the rationals, so it could neither refute this nor produce a counterexample an i64 could
// take, and `std/smt` honestly reported `unknown — no integer witness (rational-only)`.
// That honesty was the whole point of the fixture, and it is preserved: what changed is
// that the solver now closes the gap instead of reporting it.
//
// Two integer facts do it, both applied in `reduceConstraint`:
//   strictness folds away — `L < 0` on an integer L is exactly `L + 1 <= 0`
//   the constant rounds IN — with g the gcd of a row's coefficients, `row·x` is always a
//   multiple of g, so the bound tightens to `floor(-konst/g)`
//
// Neither is a heuristic: each is an equivalence over the integers, so a system it refutes
// has no integer solution. `2 * x == 3` is the smallest case — rationally satisfiable at
// x = 3/2, integer-infeasible, and now refuted with no notion of integrality in the
// elimination itself.
pub fn tighten(x: i64, y: i64): i64
requires x < y
ensures result <= y
{
    return x + 1
}

pub fn main(): i32 {
    print(tighten(2, 5))
    return 0
}
